{"records":[{"id":"dxmpmtx8zn","title":"Distinctive Genomic Features of Hydroa Vacciniforme Lymphoproliferative Disorder in Latin American Patients: A Multicenter Multi-Omics Study","description":"This study was used to determine clinicopathological features and molecular profiles of HV-LPD patients from Latin America and Mexico. We performed a clinicopathologic review of 43 HV-LPD cases from Latin America and Mexico. We also executed the first comprehensive multi-omics study of 28 Hispanic and Latin American HV-LPD patients, incorporating whole-exome and RNA sequencing, and copy number variation profiling. Analysis revealed somatic mutations, with epigenetic regulator KMT2D being the most frequently mutated gene (28%), followed by CREBBP (21.4%), and BCOR (14.3%). Inactivation of tumor suppressor genes (TSG), including ATM (14.3%), MLH1 (10.7%) and BARD1 (10.7%) were observed. Copy number analysis revealed deletions involving TSGs, MAP2K4 (50%), SMARCB1 (36%), FANCA (36%) and PRDM1 (14.2%) . Differential gene expression analysis identified significant gene overexpression in cytokine signaling, NK/T-cell activity, and JAK signaling. Analysis of serial biopsies at two different time points in three patients with HV-LPD revealed increased EBV RNA and progressive accumulation of mutations, particularly in epigenetic regulator genes including KMT2D alterations. Our findings suggest that disruption of KMT2D and other epigenetic regulators, with subsequent inactivation of tumor suppressor genes, is key in HV-LPD development in this Latin American cohort.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-09-29T06:27:53.832Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/dxmpmtx8zn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/dxmpmtx8zn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Aleksander","last_name":"Luniewski, ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Marge","last_name":"Moore","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Eli","last_name":"Williams","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Liqiang","last_name":"Xi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carlos","last_name":"Barrionuevo","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Guruprasad","last_name":" Konduru ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Pankaj  ","last_name":"Kumar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Debamita ","last_name":"Kundu ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Nihir","last_name":"Patel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Daniela ","last_name":"Duenas-Hancco ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Elizabeth ","last_name":" Cervantes","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Carlos ","last_name":" Bacchi ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Tiago ","last_name":"Vencato da Silva","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Adriana ","last_name":"Valencia ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mirna Eréndira ","last_name":"Toledo Bahena","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ana Cecilia ","last_name":" Druker","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Irma Jimena ","last_name":"Martínez Callado ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jose ","last_name":"Plaza ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Sonia ","last_name":"Toussaint Caire ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julio-Martin   ","last_name":" Sangueza ","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Alejandro","last_name":"Gru","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Ifeyinwa","last_name":"Obiorah","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":null,"name":"This study was supported by start-up funds from the Department of Pathology, University of Virginia Health, to I.E.O. ","grant_id":null}],"categories":["Lymphoma","T Cell","Epstein-Barr Virus","Childhood","Natural Killer Cell","Chronic Lymphoproliferative Disorder"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"pjfjzy4b7h","title":"Dataset and original codes for Single Cell RNA Sequencing Analysis of Oncolytic virus- and mRNA Vaccine-treated Mouse Tumors","description":"This repository contains single-cell RNA sequencing datasets and original codes used to investigate tumor microenvironment changes following treatment with an oncolytic virus (T22H07) or a mRNA vaccine (M22H04). The data include cell material and sequencing quality control criteria, marker gene expression profiles of major cell clusters, copy number variation (CNV) analysis of cancer cell clusters, gene pairs indicating cell-to-cell communication efficacy, and gene expression profiles of neutrophils and macrophages.  The codes set include PBS-treated tumor barcodes, PBS-treated tumor genes, PBS-treated tumor matrix, T22H07-treated tumor barcodes, T22H07-treated tumor genes, T22H07-treated tumor matrix, M22H04-treated tumor barcodes, M22H04-treated tumor genes, and M22H04-treated tumor matrix.  The dataset serves as supplementary information for the publication titled \"Heterologous Prime-boost with a mRNA Vaccine and an Oncolytic Virus Enhances Tumor Regression Through Overcoming Intratumoral Immune Suppression\". ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-01T07:30:40.629Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/pjfjzy4b7h","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/pjfjzy4b7h"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"kuan","last_name":"zhang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Drug Therapy for Cervix Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"cjh584h5yg","title":"Evaluation of triple negative breast cancer with heterogeneous immune infiltration","description":"This research is the first one to measure differences in immune heterogeneity within the same patient, avoiding patients' inter-heterogeneity, for the study of potential casualties to the presence of infiltrating immune cells. This difference could be explained due to the tumor intrinsic characteristics (for example, their HLA -human leukocyte antigen- phenotype having different affinity for antigens). However, here we have shown that it may depend also on the tumor heterogeneity. Secretion of different proteins and enzymes, transcriptional alterations, and expression of different regulatory genes and oncogenes by the tumor cells can lead to immune recognition and infiltration in some areas but not in others. This observation is reflected in the differential expression analysis in each patient as well as in the gene set enrichment analysis, in which, on top of the upregulation of the immune pathways in the high TIL areas, there is an upregulation of other non-immune related pathways. We hypothesized that the differences in the gene expression between the two areas are due to intrinsic differences between the tumor cells of each area that evolve differently during tumor development. The team recommends future research with single cell sequencing in patients with the same characteristics.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-01-20T10:11:13.721Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/cjh584h5yg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/cjh584h5yg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jose","last_name":"Fernandez Navarro","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Angela","last_name":"Quintana","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Enrique","last_name":"Arenas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Cristina","last_name":"Bernado","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Immunology","Oncology","Breast Cancer","Transcriptomics"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nh58jyt5dt","title":"RPL22 is a tumor suppressor in MSI-high cancers and a splicing regulator of MDM4","description":"Figures and data from the paper entitled RPL22 is a tumor suppressor in MSI-high cancers and a splicing regulator of MDM4","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-08-13T15:09:05.699Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nh58jyt5dt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nh58jyt5dt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hannah","last_name":"Weinstein","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Kevin","last_name":"Hu","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lisa ","last_name":"Fish","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Yih-An ","last_name":"Chen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Paul ","last_name":"Allegakoen","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Julia ","last_name":"Pham","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Keliana ","last_name":"Hui","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Chih-Hao ","last_name":"Chang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Meltem ","last_name":"Tutar","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lorena ","last_name":"Benitez-Rivera","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Maria ","last_name":"Baco","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hanbing ","last_name":"Song","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Andrew","last_name":"Giacomelli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Francisca ","last_name":"Vazquez","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mahmoud ","last_name":"Ghandi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Hani ","last_name":"Goodarzi","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Franklin","last_name":"Huang ","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"r5kz3kj6f2","title":"Supplementary data: Adaptation of the yeast gene knockout collection is near-perfectly predicted by fitness and diminishing return epistasis","description":"Supplementary data tables for “Adaptation of the yeast gene knockout collection is near-perfectly predicted by fitness and diminishing return epistasis”\n\nScan-o-matic growth phenotype datasets of the haploid BY4741 single gene deletion collection (MATa; his3Δ1; leu2Δ0; met15Δ0; ura3Δ0; GeneX::kanMX) adaptation to arsenite stress. From each growth curve, the colony populations doubling times (hours) have been extracted as a proxy for fitness (Zackrisson et al., 2016). \nData S1: reports dose-response growth data for gene deletion strains exposed to different degrees of arsenite stress. Data S2: reports growth data, before LOESS fitting, for the complete collection of BY4741 gene deletion strains during adaptive evolution in 3 mM of arsenite. Data S3: reports growth data, after LOESS fitting, for the complete collection of BY4741 gene deletion strains after 25, 50 and 75 generations of adaptive evolution in 3 mM of arsenite. Data S4: reports growth data, before LOESS fitting, of BY4741 gene deletion strains during adaptive evolution across a range of selection pressures. Data S5: reports growth data, after LOESS fitting, of BY4741 gene deletion strains after 25, 50 and 75 generations across a range of selection pressures. Data S6: reports growth data, of BY4741 gene deletion strains on background media before and after adaptation to a range of selection pressures. Data S7: reports growth data, of single and double mutation strains in Synthetic complete medium, and arsenite, 3 mM.\n","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-03-25T21:28:43.487Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/r5kz3kj6f2","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/r5kz3kj6f2"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Karl","last_name":"Persson","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Simon","last_name":"Stenberg","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Markus","last_name":"Tamas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jonas","last_name":"Warringer","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Evolutionary Biology","Adaptation","Yeast","Experimental Evolution"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"j46b34szhw","title":"Pharmacological Inhibition of LIF Signaling Reverses PD-L1-Mediated Immune Suppression in Gastric Cancer","description":"Gastric cancer (GC) is a major clinical challenge, characterized by limited response rates to immune checkpoint inhibitors (ICIs) and persistent immune evasion. Emerging evidence implicates Leukemia Inhibitory Factor (LIF), a cytokine of the IL-6 family, in the modulation of the tumor microenvironment, yet its contribution to PD-L1-mediated immune suppression in GC has not been  investigated. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-08-12T15:33:23.023Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/j46b34szhw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/j46b34szhw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Cristina","last_name":"Di Giorgio","orcid":null},{"first_name":"silvia","last_name":"marchianò","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Michele","last_name":"Biagioli","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Stefano","last_name":"Fiorucci","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cancer","Immunity","Gastric Disease"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"rcg4hz3trn","title":"LYZ Gene as a Novel Therapeutic Target and Diagnostic Biomarker in Glioblastoma: Insights from Multi-Omics Analysis and Functional Validation","description":"Immune checkpoint blockade is one of the current treatments for glioblastoma (GBM), which is still a very aggressive and treatment-resistant tumor of the central nervous system. This study focused on the LYZ gene in an attempt to find new therapeutic targets. We performed a thorough screening of differential gene expression between GBM and normal samples using many databases (TCGA, GTEx, GEO, and CGGA). Because LYZ is significantly upregulated in GBM tissues and is associated with shorter patient survival periods, we identified it as a gene of interest. LYZ's position on the exterior side of the plasma membrane and its participation in leukocyte-mediated immunity were identified by functional enrichment analysis, indicating a role in cell surface immune responses. Significant associations between LYZ expression and particular immune cell types were found using immune infiltration analysis, suggesting that LYZ may have an impact on the tumor microenvironment. Within GBM, single-cell research verified LYZ expression in macrophages and monocytes. LYZ was shown to express differently in GBM cell lines than in normal glial cells, according to cellular experimental verification. The LYZ gene's functional importance in the pathophysiology of GBM was highlighted by the dramatic reduction in cell proliferation, motility, and invasion that resulted from its knockout. These results suggest that LYZ is a viable therapeutic target and possible GBM diagnostic biomarker, which calls for more research into its mechanisms of action and potential clinical use.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-11-27T07:45:36.97Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/rcg4hz3trn","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/rcg4hz3trn"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Nuoyan","last_name":"Zhu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Life Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"84kjtptmd8","title":"Mesothelioma survival prediction based on a six-gene transcriptomic signature","description":"Background: Mesothelioma is an aggressive, fatal cancer that is inextricably linked to asbestos exposure. Recent trials using a combination of the immune checkpoint inhibitors ipilimumab and nivolumab has significantly improved treatment outcomes, however durable treatment responses remain restricted to a subset of patients (15-20%), highlighting the need to identify strategies that better predict treatment response.\nMethod: We performed RNAseq on a large tumor biobank (n=167) from genetically diverse mouse model, CC-MexTAg model to compare gene expression profiles of tumors from mice with different overall survival to develop a prognostic gene signature.\nResults: while the variation in gene expression data of tumors did not associate with 3-fold variation in overall survival of CC-MexTAg mice, we identified two distinct tumor clusters characterized with immune and non-immune phenotypes, in which immune cluster tumours showed the better potential of response to cancer therapies. We used 20 hub genes associated with this tumor phenotype to develop a 6-gene signature that could predict survival in four independent mesothelioma datasets (Bueno, NCI, TCGA and Creaney) and showed a potential to respond to cancer immunotherapy.\nHere, the shared data include R markdown files to perform Gene set enrichment analysis (GSEA), CIBERSORT and WGCNA on RNAseq data from CCMT mouse model (CCMT data analysis_part 1 and 2).  Folder (Gene_signature_development_validation_part 3) include the R markdown file for developing and validating the 6-gene signature via interrogating five independent human mesothelioma datasets.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2024-09-04T19:43:02.101Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/84kjtptmd8","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/84kjtptmd8"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Scott","last_name":"Fisher","orcid":null},{"first_name":"kiarash","last_name":"Behrouzfar","orcid":"0000-0002-9768-7181"}],"funders":[],"categories":["Genetics","RNA Sequencing","Mesothelioma","Mouse Model of Cancer"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8zyrz7ccbh","title":"Plant immune system activation is necessary for efficient interaction with auxin secreting beneficial bacteria ","description":"Plants continuously monitor the presence of microorganisms through their immune system to establish an adaptive response. Unlike immune recognition of pathogenic bacteria, how beneficial bacteria interact with the plant immune system is not well understood. Analysis of colonization of Arabidopsis thaliana by auxin producing beneficial bacteria revealed that activation of the plant immune system is necessary for efficient bacterial colonization and auxin secretion. A feedback loop is established in which bacterial colonization triggers an immune reaction and production of reactive oxygen species, which, in turn, stimulate auxin production by the bacteria. Auxin promotes bacterial survival and efficient root colonization, allowing the bacteria to inhibit fungal infection and promote plant health.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-07-06T12:21:06.41Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8zyrz7ccbh","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8zyrz7ccbh"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Elhanan","last_name":"Tzipilevich","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"42gtpdhhbw","title":"The interleukin-6/interleukin-23/Thelper-17-axis as a driver of neuro-immune toxicity in the major neurocognitive psychosis or deficit schizophrenia: a precision nomothetic psychiatry analysis","description":"Raw data related to the paper entitled \" The interleukin-6/interleukin-23/Thelper-17-axis as a driver of neuro-immune toxicity in the major neurocognitive psychosis or deficit schizophrenia: a precision nomothetic psychiatry analysis\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-08-08T15:48:22.134Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/42gtpdhhbw","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/42gtpdhhbw"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Hussein","last_name":"Al-Hakeim","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Medicine"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":143893,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":168,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":143893,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":143149,"restricted":70,"embargoed":674},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2347},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1403},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":689},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Centre","count":481},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":452},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":442},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"National Council for Scientific and Technological Development","count":426},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":389},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":334},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":277}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":141223},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1912},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":187},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad75ed","name":"Bicocca Open Archive Research Data","count":138},{"id":"df228bbb-ac66-410f-8c66-f61608d63d95","name":"University of South Florida","count":81},{"id":"2a9d1b7d-e447-466f-888f-aa6f84e575e4","name":"Digital Commons Data@Becker","count":66},{"id":"72c28c45-627f-446b-8a48-af7c0a51a357","name":"Università Cattolica del Sacro Cuore","count":59},{"id":"b121dfec-bb51-4f19-a2f6-bc9ce0197286","name":"Babeș-Bolyai University","count":43},{"id":"7e51a2dd-133e-43c6-a220-12c1958e202a","name":"University of Canberra","count":32},{"id":"f2ed6517-7fb4-4e02-8cef-95e75e9f0c9d","name":"Embry-Riddle Aeronautical University","count":28}],"top_institutions_stats":[{"id":"ecb64011-71f0-5223-b4e4-4d27d5f51d83","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid Centro de Biologia Molecular Severo Ochoa","count":904},{"id":"9d63ad1d-c391-5c35-a12e-cafe17017098","name":"Universidad Autonoma de Madrid","count":839},{"id":"3bfef41a-d03e-5722-bd55-dd245235fa78","name":"Bina Nusantara University","count":533},{"id":"0ad97c43-5173-56e6-bbe6-46dad5323862","name":"Daffodil International University","count":409},{"id":"55ba66b2-f276-5204-a3d8-acfa499794e0","name":"The University of Manchester","count":383}]}}